# Entities — design.md §8, and the first thing in this world that is not a node.
#
# §8 asks for "server-authoritative active objects with a registry mirroring §2:
# a visual, a collision box, and callbacks". This builds the half that pays for
# itself immediately and names the half it does not.
#
# ## What it is: dropped items
#
# §7.1 has said since M5 that "what is dropped and does not fit is lost", and
# `core/inventory.gene` names dropped-item entities as the fix. That is the
# first thing §8 buys and it is the one that closes a hole a player can see —
# dig with a full hotbar today and the node is gone.
#
# So an entity here is a stack of items at a position, and — as of §9's actions
# — an instance of a **definition** that carries a mod's `on_step`.
#
# ## The definition registry, and which of §8's four callbacks exist
#
# §8 asks for `on_activate`, `on_step`, `on_punch` and `on_death`. Two of those
# have an engine under them and two do not, and the split is not about effort:
#
# - **`on_step` is built.** The server ticks (§12), every live entity is stepped
#   once a tick, and what runs is the definition's own function. It is what
#   fills §8.1's first named absence — "a dropped item stays where it was
#   dropped, including in the air if the node under it is later dug" — and it
#   fills it *from the mod*, which is the whole argument for having an API.
# - **`on_activate` is built**, and it is one line beside `on_step`: it runs
#   when a slot is filled, which is a moment the engine already has.
# - **`on_punch` and `on_death` are not**, because nothing in this engine can
#   hit an entity. There is no ray that selects one (§7's raycast finds nodes),
#   no damage, and no health column. Adding the callbacks would be adding two
#   fields nothing could ever call, which is a worse kind of absence than a
#   stated one.
#
# This was blocked until this commit for a reason that turned out not to exist:
# §D7.17 recorded "the VM rejects a nominal type inside a `Callback`
# annotation". The VM rejects **every** `Callback`, primitives included, and
# always had the capability under the name `Fn`. See §D7.17 for the measurement.
#
# ## Positions are F64 and everything else is an id
#
# §1's `Vec3` — continuous position, unlike a node. That is the whole reason
# entities are a separate system rather than a node property: a node is at an
# integer coordinate by construction and an item on the ground is at 0.5 above
# it, between two others.
#
# The columns are `F64` rather than `F32` for once, because a position is
# compared against a player's own `F64` position (§7) and rounding one side of
# that comparison would make a pickup radius asymmetric.

(mod entity ^profile web)

# The world an `on_step` reads, imported for the same reason `core/abm.gene`
# imports it and nothing else: a callback's signature has to name it, and
# naming `Game` instead would close an import cycle through `core/api.gene`.
# What the mod needs beyond the world, it captures.
(import [World] from "./loaded.gene")

(let max_entities 256.0)
(let max_entity_defs 32.0)

# §1 reserves three content ids so that `air` means what §1 says whether or not
# a mod declared it; §8's definitions reserve one for the same reason. The
# engine spawns a dropped item from `core/inventory.gene`'s overflow path
# without asking a mod first, so there has to be a definition to spawn it as
# before any mod has run. `register_entity` under this name replaces its
# callbacks rather than adding a second definition — which is how the mod in
# this repository gives dropped items their physics.
(let item_def_name "item")
(let entity_item 0.0)
# §8: "The player is not an entity in M5; making it one is a refactor M8 should
# do deliberately." This is that kind. A player entity is server-owned like any
# other — a position, a visual, and a slot — and the thing it buys is the one
# §8.1 named as the clearest statement of what §8 still owed: **a player can see
# another player.**
#
# It is a kind rather than a separate system because everything a player needs
# on the wire is what a dropped item needs: an id, a position, and something to
# draw. What differs is size and that it is never picked up, and both of those
# are one comparison against this number.
(let entity_player 1.0)

# What an entity's `on_step` is given: the world to read, the slot it is, and
# how long the step was in seconds. Everything else it captures — `game` above
# all, which is what `move_entity` and `remove_entity` need.
#
# The slot rather than the id, because a slot is what every accessor here takes
# and resolving an id costs a scan. A slot is stable for as long as the entity
# is alive, which is exactly the window a step runs in.

(type EntityRegistry
  ^props {^kind   (Buffer F32)
          ^item   (Buffer F32)   # for `entity_item`: what it is a stack of
          ^count  (Buffer F32)
          ^x      (Buffer F64)
          ^y      (Buffer F64)
          ^z      (Buffer F64)
          ^def    (Buffer F32)   # which definition this slot is an instance of
          # Zero means the slot is free. Ids are 1-based for exactly this, so a
          # freed slot cannot be mistaken for entity 0.
          ^id     (Buffer F32)
          ^n      (Buffer F32)   # two cells: slots used, next id
          # §8's definition registry — the mod's half.
          ^def_names    (List Str)
          ^def_step     (List (Fn [World F64 F64] Nil))
          ^def_activate (List (Fn [World F64] Nil))
          ^def_n        (Buffer F32)}

  (ctor []
    (set! self/kind ($buffer F32 max_entities))
    (set! self/item ($buffer F32 max_entities))
    (set! self/count ($buffer F32 max_entities))
    (set! self/x ($buffer F64 max_entities))
    (set! self/y ($buffer F64 max_entities))
    (set! self/z ($buffer F64 max_entities))
    (set! self/def ($buffer F32 max_entities))
    (set! self/id ($buffer F32 max_entities))
    (set! self/n ($buffer F32 2.0))
    (set! self/def_names [])
    (set! self/def_step [])
    (set! self/def_activate [])
    (set! self/def_n ($buffer F32 1.0))))

(let cell_used 0.0)
(let cell_next 1.0)

# The no-op a reserved definition carries until a mod replaces it. An entity
# whose definition does nothing is exactly what M8 shipped, so this is also the
# behaviour the engine falls back to rather than a special case in the tick.
(fn no_step [world : World slot : F64 dt : F64] : Nil nil)
(fn no_activate [world : World slot : F64] : Nil nil)

(fn new_entities [] : EntityRegistry
  (var e (new EntityRegistry))
  (e/n ~ set! cell_next 1.0)
  (add_entity_def e item_def_name no_step no_activate)
  e)

# --- definitions --------------------------------------------------------------

(type EntityDefsFull
  ^props {^message Str}
  (ctor [message : Str] (set! self/message message)))

(impl Error for EntityDefsFull)

(fn entity_def_count [e : EntityRegistry] : F64 (e/def_n ~ get 0.0))

# **The last registration of a name wins**, which is why this scans backwards.
# A definition is appended rather than overwritten, so registering `item` gives
# the reserved one a successor instead of editing it in place — and lookup by
# name finds the successor. Two things fall out and both are wanted: a mod can
# furnish a reserved definition without the engine having a mutation path for
# callbacks, and when §9's loader orders mods by `depends`, the later mod is the
# one whose definition answers.
#
# An entity already spawned keeps the index it was spawned with, because `^def`
# is a column on the slot. That is also right: a re-registration should describe
# the next item that drops, not silently re-animate the ones already lying on
# the ground.
#
# It scans backwards rather than overwriting because the web profile has no
# `set!` on a `(List (Fn …))` — a real constraint, and the design it forced is
# better than the one it refused.
(fn entity_def_named [e : EntityRegistry name : Str] : F64
  (var names e/def_names)
  (var found -1.0)
  (var i (entity_def_count e))
  (while (> i 0.0)
    (set i (- i 1.0))
    (var j i)
    (if_yes (&& (== found -1.0) (== names/%j name))
      (set found i)))
  found)

(fn add_entity_def [e : EntityRegistry name : Str
                    on_step : (Fn [World F64 F64] Nil)
                    on_activate : (Fn [World F64] Nil)] : F64
  (var id (entity_def_count e))
  (if_yes (>= id max_entity_defs)
    (fail (new EntityDefsFull
               $"entity definitions are full at ${max_entity_defs}")))
  (e/def_names ~ push! name)
  (e/def_step ~ push! on_step)
  (e/def_activate ~ push! on_activate)
  (e/def_n ~ set! 0.0 (+ id 1.0))
  id)

(fn entity_def_name_of [e : EntityRegistry def : F64] : Str
  (var names e/def_names)
  (var i def)
  names/%i)

(fn entity_def_step [e : EntityRegistry def : F64] : (Fn [World F64 F64] Nil)
  (var steps e/def_step)
  (var i def)
  steps/%i)

(fn entity_def_activate [e : EntityRegistry def : F64] : (Fn [World F64] Nil)
  (var acts e/def_activate)
  (var i def)
  acts/%i)

(fn entity_def_of [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : F64 (e/def ~ get slot))

# The high-water mark of occupied slots, which is what a caller iterates. Slots
# below it may be free — `entity_alive?` is the test — and compacting instead
# would renumber live entities, which the wire cannot afford.
(fn entity_slots [e : EntityRegistry] : F64
  (e/n ~ get cell_used))

(fn entity_alive? [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : Bool
  (> (e/id ~ get slot) 0.0))

(fn entity_id [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : F64 (e/id ~ get slot))
(fn entity_kind [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : F64 (e/kind ~ get slot))
(fn entity_item_of [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : F64 (e/item ~ get slot))
(fn entity_count [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : F64 (e/count ~ get slot))
(fn entity_x [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : F64 (e/x ~ get slot))
(fn entity_y [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : F64 (e/y ~ get slot))
(fn entity_z [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : F64 (e/z ~ get slot))

(fn live_entities [e : EntityRegistry] : F64
  (var n 0.0)
  (var i 0.0)
  (var slots (entity_slots e))
  (while (< i slots)
    (if_yes (entity_alive? e i) (set n (+ n 1.0)))
    (set i (+ i 1.0)))
  n)

# --- spawning ----------------------------------------------------------------

# A free slot, reusing a dead one before growing. Answers -1 when the table is
# full, which a caller must handle: silently dropping a spawn would put this
# system back where §7.1 was before it existed.
(fn free_slot [e : EntityRegistry] : F64
  (var found -1.0)
  (var slots (entity_slots e))
  (var i 0.0)
  (while (< i slots)
    (if_yes (&& (== found -1.0) (! (entity_alive? e i)))
      (set found i))
    (set i (+ i 1.0)))
  (if (>= found 0.0) found
    (if (< slots max_entities)
      (do (e/n ~ set! cell_used (+ slots 1.0)) slots)
      -1.0)))

(fn spawn_item [e : EntityRegistry item : F64 count : F64
                x : F64 y : F64 z : F64] : F64
  (var slot (free_slot e))
  (if (< slot 0.0) -1.0
    (do
      (var id (e/n ~ get cell_next))
      (e/n ~ set! cell_next (+ id 1.0))
      (e/kind ~ set! slot entity_item)
      (e/item ~ set! slot item)
      (e/count ~ set! slot count)
      (e/x ~ set! slot x)
      (e/y ~ set! slot y)
      (e/z ~ set! slot z)
      (e/def ~ set! slot (entity_def_named e item_def_name))
      (e/id ~ set! slot id)
      id)))

# What an `on_step` moves. A continuous position, unclamped: an item that walks
# off the world is a mod's bug and hiding it inside the setter would make it an
# engine mystery instead.
(fn move_entity [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64 x : F64 y : F64 z : F64] : Nil
  (e/x ~ set! slot x)
  (e/y ~ set! slot y)
  (e/z ~ set! slot z))

# A player, spawned into the same table as everything else. `item` carries what
# to draw it as — `mods/default` registers a `miclone:player` node for exactly
# this, so the client's existing item-cube path draws a player with no new
# vertex format and no new message (§8.3).
(fn spawn_player [e : EntityRegistry visual : F64
                  x : F64 y : F64 z : F64] : F64
  (var slot (free_slot e))
  (if (< slot 0.0) -1.0
    (do
      (var id (e/n ~ get cell_next))
      (e/n ~ set! cell_next (+ id 1.0))
      (e/kind ~ set! slot entity_player)
      (e/item ~ set! slot visual)
      # A player is one of itself. The count is what a dropped stack uses and
      # what a removal sets to 0, so a player carries 1 to stay on the same
      # add/move/remove rule as everything else (§10).
      (e/count ~ set! slot 1.0)
      (e/x ~ set! slot x)
      (e/y ~ set! slot y)
      (e/z ~ set! slot z)
      (e/def ~ set! slot (entity_def_named e item_def_name))
      (e/id ~ set! slot id)
      id)))

# The web profile exports functions, not `let` bindings — the same reason
# `core/protocol.gene` has `kind_*` and `core/formspec.gene` has element-kind
# accessors. A `.mjs` peer needs to tell a player from a dropped stack.
(fn kind_item [] : F64 entity_item)
(fn kind_player [] : F64 entity_player)

(fn entity_is_player? [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : Bool
  (== (e/kind ~ get slot) entity_player))

(fn despawn [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64] : Nil
  (e/id ~ set! slot 0.0)
  (e/count ~ set! slot 0.0))

(fn slot_of_id [e : EntityRegistry id : F64] : F64
  (var found -1.0)
  (var slots (entity_slots e))
  (var i 0.0)
  (while (< i slots)
    (if_yes (&& (== found -1.0) (== (e/id ~ get i) id))
      (set found i))
    (set i (+ i 1.0)))
  found)

# --- pickup ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# §8's whole reason for existing here. The radius is generous — an item you can
# see at your feet should be one you have — and it is squared at the comparison
# so nothing takes a square root per entity per tick.

(let pickup_radius 1.6)

(fn within_pickup? [e : EntityRegistry slot : F64
                    px : F64 py : F64 pz : F64] : Bool
  (var dx (- (entity_x e slot) px))
  (var dy (- (entity_y e slot) py))
  (var dz (- (entity_z e slot) pz))
  (<= (+ (* dx dx) (+ (* dy dy) (* dz dz)))
      (* pickup_radius pickup_radius)))
